# English-first for version-controlled files

**MANDATORY.** Every file tracked by git in this repository must be written primarily in English. This keeps commits
readable on GitHub, supports international contributors, and keeps the codebase in one consistent language. This rule is
not optional and must not be weakened for convenience.

## Scope: git-tracked files only

- In scope: source, templates, styles, tests, docs, `.mdc` rules, configuration, comments, identifiers, UI copy and
  commit messages that land in the repository.
- Determine scope with `git ls-files` and `git status --short`. A tracked file being modified (`M`) or a file staged for
  addition (`A`) is in scope.
- **Exempt**: local temporary files that are not under version control — anything ignored
  by [.gitignore](mdc:.gitignore) or a parent `.gitignore` (e.g. `node_modules/`, `dist/`, `.angular/`, local logs,
  screenshots, scratch notes, IDE-local plans and user-level config). They may use any language and must never be added
  to the repository just to satisfy this rule. Use `git check-ignore -v <path>` when in doubt.
- A file becomes in-scope the moment it moves from ignored/untracked into git: translate its content **before** staging
  it.

## What "primarily English" means

- Identifiers, file names, comments and JSDoc, UI labels and messages, test names, error messages, documentation
  headings, rule bodies and commit messages are in English.
- Allowed exceptions: proper nouns, brand names, third-party URLs, snippets quoted verbatim from external documentation,
  and data values that must match an external system.
- Existing Chinese content in tracked files is **technical debt, not an exception**. Whenever such a file is edited,
  convert its touched lines and that file's comments/labels to English in the same change. A full translation pass of
  untouched files is a separate, explicitly requested task.

## Why

A repository shared on GitHub is read by international maintainers. Mixed Chinese/English identifiers, comments, UI copy
and commit messages hurt grep-ability, tooling and AI context, and make reviews harder. Consistency beats partial
localization.

## Verification before delivery

- `git status --short` shows only the intended files.
- For each changed tracked file, grep for CJK characters (`[\u4e00-\u9fff]`); every hit must be one of the allowed
  exceptions above.

